---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import index from '../data/knowledgeIndex.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const internal = (segment) => `${base}/${segment.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
const requireObject = (id) => {
  const item = index.objects.find((candidate) => candidate.id === id);
  if (!item) throw new Error(`Publications projection is missing canonical object ${id}`);
  return item;
};

const authorialCapacity = requireObject('object:authorial-capacity');
const epistemicHandoff = requireObject('object:epistemic-handoff');

const published = [
  ['001', authorialCapacity.title, 'Exploratory synthesis / working paper', authorialCapacity.status, internal(authorialCapacity.href)],
];
---
<Layout
  title="Publications"
  description="Papers, working research objects, negative results and other durable outputs from Agalmic Research."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Publications</p>
      <h1>Work that is ready to be read, used or challenged.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Not every useful result needs to become a conventional paper. This page collects research that has reached a durable public form, including papers, methods, datasets, software and negative results.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Released</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Current public research.</h2>
          <p>Each item keeps its status visible so exploratory work is not mistaken for a settled conclusion.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="index-list">
        {published.map(([n, title, type, status, href]) => (
          <article class="index-item">
            <span class="index-meta">{n}</span>
            <div>
              <h3><a href={href}>{title}</a></h3>
              <p class="muted">{type}</p>
            </div>
            <span class="index-status">{status}</span>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current empirical work</p>
        <h2>Attention Allocation Under Cognitive Abundance.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The first version produced a useful negative result before the outcome data were opened: the available sample was too small to answer the planned question with enough precision.</p>
        <p>A larger follow-up is currently limited by trustworthy historical data. A future publication may therefore take the form of a result, a methods contribution, a dataset, or a documented limit rather than a positive finding.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href={internal('projects')}>See the project</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('results')}>See results and corrections</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Work in progress</p>
        <h2>Exploratory drafts stay visibly exploratory.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><strong>{epistemicHandoff.title}</strong> is an exploratory draft with status <em>{epistemicHandoff.status}</em>.</p>
        <p>Other early ideas remain in the wider research record until there is enough evidence or argument to justify a more durable release.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal(epistemicHandoff.href)}>Open the draft</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('explore')}>Explore the research record</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Supporting detail</p>
        <h2>Evidence and history remain available.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Readers who want to inspect prior work, corrections, contribution history or research methods can follow the deeper records without requiring every visitor to learn the internal vocabulary first.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('lineage')}>Prior work and lineage</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('provenance')}>Provenance</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('method')}>Methods</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
